   "texts": {
    "profile": "## The four long-term causes and the one spark\n- Historians often describe four long-term causes of the First World War, plus one event that set it off.\n- The spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.\n\n## Militarism as the first cause\n- In the years before 1914 the great powers built up large armies and navies.\n- Germany and Britain raced to build battleships.\n- Generals began to plan for war.\n- Many leaders came to see war as a normal way to settle disputes.\n- Think of it as an analogy for militarism: players enlarge their forces all season and then treat the contest as routine.\n\n## Alliances as the second cause\n- Europe was divided into two blocs.\n- The Triple Entente joined France, Russia and Britain.\n- The Triple Alliance joined Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy.\n- Because of these alliances, a local conflict could pull in many countries.\n- A quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war.\n- Think of it as an analogy for the alliances: in a two-team league, a scuffle between two players obliges every teammate to join.\n\n## Imperialism and nationalism as the third and fourth causes\n- Imperialism means rivalry between empires for colonies, markets and power.\n- The European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia, which increased distrust between them.\n- Think of it as an analogy for imperialism: two artists racing to claim the same pages begin to distrust one another.\n- Nationalism is strong pride in one's nation or a wish for independence.\n- Nationalism was especially powerful in the Balkans.\n- Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples and Austria-Hungary feared losing its empire.\n\n## The spark, step by step\n- Step 1: Before the step, the alliances meant a local conflict could pull in many countries.\n- Step 2: Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, is assassinated in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.\n- Step 3: Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia follows the spark, and the alliances remain in place.\n- Step 4: Because of the alliances, Russia, Germany, France and Britain are drawn into the war.\n- The result is checked as the material states it: a quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war.\n- The material answers a harder question: Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia drew in Russia, Germany, France and Britain because of the alliances.",
    "mirror": "## Four long-term causes, plus one spark\n- The text gives four long-term causes of the war and one single event that set it off.\n- Layout: CAUSES → 1 militarism, 2 alliances, 3 imperialism, 4 nationalism; then the SPARK → the assassination in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.\n\n## Militarism\n- Militarism is the first cause: before 1914 the great powers built up large armies and navies.\n- Germany and Britain raced each other to build battleships.\n- Generals began to plan for war, and many leaders saw war as a normal way to settle disputes.\n- Think of militarism like two basketball teams both signing bigger and bigger players before tip-off, because that pictures the build-up of armies and navies.\n\n## Alliances\n- Alliances are the second cause, and they split Europe into two blocs.\n- The Triple Entente joined France, Russia and Britain.\n- The Triple Alliance joined Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy.\n- Layout: BLOK 1 → Triple Entente: France, Russia, Britain; BLOK 2 → Triple Alliance: Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy.\n- Chain: a local conflict → alliances pull in many countries → a quarrel between two states grows into a continental war.\n- Think of alliances like a video game squad, where one player's fight drags the whole party in, just as a local conflict could pull in many countries here.\n\n## Imperialism\n- Imperialism is rivalry between empires for colonies, markets and power.\n- The European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia.\n- That competition increased distrust between the empires.\n\n## Nationalism and the spark\n- Nationalism means strong pride in your nation, or a wish for independence.\n- Nationalism was especially powerful in the Balkans.\n- There, Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples, and Austria-Hungary feared losing its empire.\n- Think of nationalism like a comic artist who wants their own series to stand alone, matching the wish for independence in the definition.\n- The spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.\n- Because of the alliances, Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia soon drew in Russia, Germany, France and Britain."
